WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.530
You let an AI agent trade with your money.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.570
What stops it from going too far?

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:16.610
Mandate Layer gives it an allowance card: fifty per trade, a hundred and fifty in total, three times leverage, two assets, seven days.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.240
A contract on Base checks the card on every order.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:28.980
Inside those limits, it works alone: it buys market data for a cent or less per request, and opens a small trade.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.835
Then it tries eighty.

00:00:31.835 --> 00:00:33.165
The card says fifty.

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:34.560
The contract says no.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.780
From a recorded run: the order was really sent, and reverted on chain.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:49.980
The wallet has a second wall: a Base Spend Permission lets the agent refill its card, at most a hundred a day.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.274
Sixty: fine.

00:00:51.274 --> 00:00:55.051
Fifty more the same day makes a hundred and ten.

00:00:55.051 --> 00:00:55.680
Refused.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.585
That was a second recorded run.

00:00:58.585 --> 00:01:01.350
Coinbase's own contract reverted it on chain.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.639
Then the user revokes the card.

00:01:04.639 --> 00:01:08.480
The next order, small and inside every limit, is refused.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.090
The first run's last step: reverted on chain, after revocation.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.854
The agent can trade; the contract decides how far.

00:01:18.854 --> 00:01:24.910
This is the PepeFi codebase, deployed separately for the Colosseum hackathon's Base track.
